Given below are an itemized list of contents in the repository:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 1. annual_pv_waste_el.pkl: A .pkl file created using Python\'92s pickle module for converting the list of annual early loss PV waste quantities into a binary format \
2. annual_pv_waste_avg.pkl: A .pkl file created using Python\'92s pickle module for converting the list of annual average (of early and regular loss numbers) PV waste quantities into a binary format \
3. annual_pv_waste_rl.pkl: A .pkl file created using Python\'92s pickle module for converting the list of annual regular loss PV waste quantities into a binary format \
4. Parameter Data and Sources.xlsx: Describes source data used namely. Tab 1 is the cover sheet that specifies the contents of the spreadsheet. Tab 2 provides End of Use solar panel projected waste quantities (in metric tons) from 2020 - 2050. Tab 3 provides expected domestic material demands (in metric tons) for a range of manufacturing raw materials in 2030. Tab 4 compiles a range of primary and secondary material costs, lanfilling costs and transportation costs based on literature sources. Tab 5 compiles individual material recovery and yield rates based on literature sources. Tab 6 provides Life Cycle Assessment metrics compiled from preceding studies.\
5. PV SMM ABM Tool 2025_AC and NM.ipynb: Is the code for the actual ABM model itself and is accessible via Jupyter Notebook. \
\
The model requires as input the following information (refer to repository files 1 through 4): 1. EoU product collection data from which critical materials may be recovered 2. Material demands by partner firms 3. Material costs, shipping costs, disposal costs 4. Recovery process cost and yields 5. Partner firm behavioral factors such as confidence in use of secondary materials, tendency for opportunistic negotiation regarding cost sharing 6. Firm locations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This model generates the following outputs (under tested conditions): 1. Critical material recovery potential 2. Net financial benefits projections for firms participating in the secondary materials market 3. Potential for net avoided environmental impacts. \
